{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016cmbyrkUhS58pdXXwpZ6aZ","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (32.7KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84MM5EGYN6XYF3NM1Q9Q5YR/aae58b53-08ac-4143-a1f0-0a08fb816188/tool-results/btl63ul5f.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\nc5a17ab Address #15 review: pool wiring, redaction ctx, test flakes\ndocs/TODO.md:32:- #221 Scanner failure streaks are visible on the status issue\ndocs/TODO.md:42:- #58 Status issue daily digest\ndocs/TODO.md:49:- #205 Reviewer aid step: run pinned deterministic tools before a reviewer agent\ndocs/proposal.md:85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dd70424e-bb5d-4404-a4cd-76ee3bc1de52","uuid":"8a94455b-f57e-49c9-82fb-fcb668268148","timestamp":"2026-11-23T16:43:07.854Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"c5a17ab Address #15 review: pool wiring, redaction ctx, test flakes\ndocs/TODO.md:32:- #221 Scanner failure streaks are visible on the status issue\ndocs/TODO.md:42:- #58 Status issue daily digest\ndocs/TODO.md:49:- #205 Reviewer aid step: run pinned deterministic tools before a reviewer agent\ndocs/proposal.md:85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\ndocs/proposal.md:156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`.\ndocs/proposal.md:188:**Each scanner has its own state, and GitHub is the truth.** Every scanner keeps a separate SQLite file, `~/.ard/state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`: fingerprint to issue, first and last seen, suppressions, last run. Scanners never share or lock each other's state, and one can be reset without touching the rest. The controller keeps its own jobs, attempts, events and a small key/value table (for example the status issue number) in `_controller.db` in the same directory, and dry-run uses `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/`. Owner and repo are lowercased (GitHub names are case-insensitive) and nested, so distinct repos never share a directory (`a-b/c` and `a/b-c` would collide as `a-b-c`); `ard serve` and `ard rebuild-state` refuse to start while a pre-#82 flat `<owner>-<repo>` state directory exists under `state/` or `state-dryrun/`, and ard does not migrate it (decided, #82). Every issue body carries hidden markers, so `ard rebuild-state <scanner>` restores a database from GitHub after a lost disk or a host move. The body holds a header `<!-- ard:scanner=<name> group=<enc> -->` and a checklist between `<!-- ard:findings -->` and `<!-- /ard:findings -->`, one line per finding: `- [ ] <title> <!-- ard:fp=<enc> sev=<severity> [reg=<old issue>] -->`. `<enc>` percent-encodes every byte outside `[A-Za-z0-9._:/@+=,~-]`, so the proposal's example fingerprint stays readable and nothing can close the comment. A ticked box means the finding is gone. Per-finding details sit in a second span, `<!-- ard:details -->`, cut to 2,000 characters each and trimmed from the end to keep the body under 60,000; `issue.md` holds them untruncated. The controller reads and rewrites only the two spans, so text a human or triage adds elsewhere survives. A human who deletes a marker line makes that finding look new, which is accepted because GitHub is the truth.\ndocs/proposal.md:260:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\ndocs/proposal.md:275:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\ndocs/proposal.md:322:3. **Run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, and says which findings are resolved.\ndocs/proposal.md:324:5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold` or `ard:tests:failed` label is present; it never converts a PR back to draft and never merges, which stays with the merger (Phase 2) and humans.\ndocs/proposal.md:325:6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) re-verifies, with `prgate.Covers` against the reviewed head recorded by the review's claim, that no code of the PR changed since before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\ndocs/proposal.md:418:Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts and 3 review rounds. Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day`, `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\ndocs/proposal.md:422:**Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\ndocs/proposal.md:428:- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write). The merger reads check runs (checks: read) and the branch rules (metadata); commit statuses are not read. The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\ndocs/proposal.md:433:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\ndocs/proposal.md:439:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\ndocs/proposal.md:440:- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. At the cap the controller stops claiming and posts once on the status issue.\ndocs/proposal.md:442:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a se","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M84MM5EGYN6XYF3NM1Q9Q5YR/aae58b53-08ac-4143-a1f0-0a08fb816188/tool-results/btl63ul5f.txt","persistedOutputSize":33518},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016cmbyrkUhS58pdXXwpZ6aZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
